# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANCHORING POLICY -- read before adding a pattern.
#
# A pattern with no slash matches at EVERY depth. Three times that has silently
# excluded real work: `reference/` also matched docs/reference/, `state/` also
# matched src/hammunition/state/. Both looked obviously fine.
#
# So: a pattern naming a project-root artifact is anchored with a leading slash.
# A pattern that must match at any depth -- secrets, caches, third-party
# archives, editor cruft -- is fine unanchored, and is listed by name with its
# reason in scripts/audit_gitignore.py. That script fails on any pattern that is
# neither, and separately fails if anything in the source tree is ignored.
# Run it: `python3 scripts/audit_gitignore.py`. CI runs it too.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reference material — third-party source we study but never redistribute.
# AHRL tarballs, upstream releases, extracted trees. Provenance stays clean.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anchored to the repo root: docs/reference/ is a required documentation
# section (see CLAUDE.md) and must NOT be caught by this rule.
/reference/
/vendor/
!/reference/README.md
!/reference/.gitkeep

# Archives and images of any kind
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.tar.bz2
*.tar.xz
*.zip
*.7z
*.rar
*.gz
*.bz2
*.xz
*.iso
*.img
*.deb
*.rpm
*.AppImage
*.snap
*.flatpak
*.dmg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets and machine-specific config — nothing here ever gets committed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.asc
!**/pubkey.asc
*.gpg
!**/*.pub.gpg
secrets.yml
secrets.yaml
*_rsa
*_ed25519
.netrc
.credentials

# Operator-specific station config (callsigns, grid squares, rig paths)
station.local.yml
station.local.yaml
*.local.yml
*.local.yaml
!example.local.yml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
/build/
/develop-eggs/
/dist/
/downloads/
/eggs/
.eggs/
/lib64/
/parts/
/sdist/
/var/
/wheels/
share/python-wheels/
*.egg-info/
/.installed.cfg
*.egg
# Anchored: unanchored, `MANIFEST` matches any file or directory of that name at
# any depth. It is a setuptools artifact and only ever appears at the root.
/MANIFEST

# Environments. No trailing slash on the two names a virtualenv actually gets:
# `.venv/` matches only a *directory*, so a worktree whose `.venv` is a symlink
# to the main checkout's venv was not ignored, `git add -A` took it, and commit
# 0e90998 shipped a symlink carrying an absolute home-directory path to main.
# CI stayed green. A test now builds that symlink in a scratch repo against
# this file and asserts it is ignored.
.venv
venv
ENV/
env/
.python-version

# Tooling caches
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.dmypy.json
dmypy.json
.ruff_cache/
.tox/
.nox/
/.coverage
/.coverage.*
/htmlcov/
/coverage.xml
*.cover
.hypothesis/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime state — transaction logs, caches, generated artifacts
#
# ANCHORED to the repo root. `state/` unanchored also matched
# src/hammunition/state/ — a source package — and silently kept it out of git
# through a whole commit. Same class of bug as the unanchored `reference/` rule.
# Caches that legitimately appear at any depth (__pycache__, .venv, .mypy_cache)
# stay unanchored on purpose. A test asserts every source package is tracked.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/state/
/logs/
# `*.log` stays unanchored -- a run writes its log wherever it was run from -- and
# is therefore exactly the shape that swallowed `reference/` and `state/`. The
# working-tree half of scripts/audit_gitignore.py is what makes that safe: a .log
# appearing under a source root is a failure, not a silent exclusion.
*.log
.hammunition/
/catalog.cache
/coverage-report.json
/coverage-report.md

# Generated udev rules (produced by the engine, never hand-edited into git)
/generated/
*.rules
!catalog/hardware/**/*.rules

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Docs build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/site/
docs/_build/
.docusaurus/
.cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Containers and test scratch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.container-cache/
/test-results/
*.qcow2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors and OS cruft
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
.vscode/
!.vscode/extensions.json
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.direnv/
